    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since we know $\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ$, we can consider the triangle $QRS$.\nIn triangle $QRS$, the sum of the interior angles is $180^\\circ$.\nSince we know two of the angles, we can find the third angle.\n$\\angle RQS+\\angle QSR+\\angle QRS=180^\\circ$.\n$\\angle RQS + \\angle QSR + 80^\\circ = 180^\\circ$.\n$\\angle RQS + \\angle QSR = 100^\\circ$.\nSince $RSP$ is a straight line, we know that $\\angle RQSP = 180^\\circ$.\nSince $\\angle QSR+\\angle RQS = \\angle RQSP$, we can find $\\angle QSR$.\n$\\angle QSR = \\angle RQSP - \\angle RQS$.\n$\\angle QSR = 180^\\circ - \\angle RQS - 80^\\circ$.\n$\\angle QSR = 100^\\circ - \\angle RQS$.\nSubstituting this into the equation $\\angle RQS + \\angle QSR = 100^\\circ$:\n$\\angle RQS + (100^\\circ - \\angle RQS) = 100^\\circ$.\nThis equation simplifies to $0^\\circ = 0^\\circ$, which is true for any value of $\\angle RQS$.\nTherefore, we can conclude that $\\angle PQR = \\angle QRS$.\nSince $\\angle QRS = \\angle QSR$ and $\\angle QSR = \\angle RQS$, we have $\\angle PQR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times (180^\\circ - \\angle QSR)$.\nWe know that $\\angle QSR = 100^\\circ - \\angle RQS$, so $\\angle PQR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times (180^\\circ - (100^\\circ - \\angle RQS))$.\nSimplifying further, we get $\\angle PQR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times (80^\\circ + \\angle RQS)$.\nTherefore, $\\angle PQR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times (80^\\circ + \\angle RQS) = \\boxed{26.67^\\circ}$.\nThe answer is: 26.67",
